WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.000
So really we're on the brink,
I think, of a transformation

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.160
in the way science is done.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.160
Not only tackling the
problem of climate change

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:11.640
and trying to find solutions,

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:14.040
but also actually trying to
understand the system itself.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:25.160
So some of the technological advances
that we might be able to use

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.320
to combat climate change
involve the ability

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.720
to generate energy
in sustainable ways.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:42.760
The question today is can we make
solar panels or other materials

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.360
that are capable of generating
energy in useful ways?

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.760
In the past, scientists and engineers
have developed new materials,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.440
but in rather a haphazard way.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.560
Thomas Edison created the lightbulb

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.760
but he developed
the original filament

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:03.480
by testing thousands of materials to
find eventually the one that worked.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:08.680
Today we can use machine
learning and artificial intelligence

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.360
in our search for new materials.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.680
The reason that we can
do it better in the computer

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:19.520
is that the computer allows
millions of potential solutions

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.640
to be searched in ways that
you couldn't do in the lab.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.320
The reason that AI is important in
the development of our understanding

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:37.680
of the Earth's system is that
we're getting more and more data

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.000
about the Earth as time goes on.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.960
So today we collect satellite data,

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.080
remote sensing observations
allow us now

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.120
to look at the patterns
of landslides.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.440
As climate is changing,
as weather patterns change,

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.480
is the land being more destabilised
by excess water?

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.680
Or are the erosion patterns
in the mountains

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.640
changing as climate changes?

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.680
Extreme weather events
as a result of climate change

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.840
do appear to be on the increase.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.480
But there's another aspect of this -
cities are getting bigger,

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.920
areas that have always been
vulnerable to natural hazards.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.600
With so many people
potentially at risk,

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.520
it becomes important to understand
the system very quickly.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:28.720
Examples include
understanding the ways in which

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.040
to respond to a situation.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.280
Where to send the first responders.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.000
Which hospital is put on high alert.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:40.640
Where to send relief supplies,
who needs tents.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.680
So all of these sorts of
disaster relief processes

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.120
depend upon information.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:54.080
And we know, as we travel
around congested cities,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:57.640
that we spend a lot
of time sitting in traffic,

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.720
burning energy, getting nowhere.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.160
Autonomous vehicles provide routes

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.000
to making transport systems
more effective, more efficient,

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.040
so that you don't have these waves
of static traffic on a motorway.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.280
That everything is moving
at the right speed

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.760
and gets from one place
to another in the most effective way,

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.240
the most efficient way.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.560
That's not something that,
as individuals,

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.560
we're very good at
planning for ourselves.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.960
So if we want to reduce, recycle,
reuse, can AI help us?

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.480
Well maybe. But it depends
upon our motivation.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:39.240
AI is a tool, it's not a master,
and so our responses ultimately

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:43.640
will depend upon our own
personal motivations,

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.680
and those of the society
that we're part of.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.440
But AI and machine learning can
help us move in the right direction.

